/*
 * MetAcsl security properties (docs/phase0-implementation-plan.md step I9), generated by
 * scripts/security_props.py from the structured threat model. Do not edit: regenerate.
 *
 * run_wp_function.py --security-props writes this preamble, then every property with an instance in
 * the function under proof, to a meta file parsed next to the target sources, and runs
 * `-meta -then-last -wp ...`. MetAcsl resolves the names below in the linked program, so the meta file
 * needs no declarations. Every generated annotation is named `<property>: meta:`, which maps its WP
 * goals to the property's ledger class (run_wp_function.goal_obligation_class).
 *
 * Vocabulary (plan step S8): the properties use the canonical names of
 * templates/acsl/acsl_vocabulary.h and define none of their own. The meta file is a translation unit
 * of its own and does not include that header (a target need not), so it takes `strlen` from
 * __fc_libc (security_props.meta_file_text puts `#include "__fc_string_axiomatic.h"` above this
 * preamble: the templates stay pure ACSL) and repeats `no_crlf_string` with the vocabulary's exact
 * text: Frama-C merges identical
 * definitions across files, so this one and the vocabulary's coexist (checked in the image). Keep the
 * two texts identical. A length-bounded buffer is the vocabulary's `no_crlf_or_nul(buf, len)`.
 * MetAcsl properties only reach WP; E-ACSL never runs them (it can execute `no_crlf_string`, through
 * `strlen`, when a contract names it).
 */
//# preamble
/*@
  predicate no_crlf_string(char *s) =
    \forall integer i; 0 <= i < strlen(s) ==> (s[i] != '\r' && s[i] != '\n');
*/
